<h2>What's Genuinely Cheaper in Abu Dhabi</h2>
<p>A few categories where Abu Dhabi residents consistently report lower costs than equivalent Dubai neighborhoods:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Mid-range apartment rent outside the central islands</strong> — areas like Khalifa City or Mohammed Bin Zayed City tend to offer more space per dirham than Dubai's equivalent commuter suburbs.</li>
  <li><strong>Parking</strong> — paid parking is less pervasive and generally cheaper than central Dubai, especially away from the Corniche and central business districts.</li>
  <li><strong>Government service fees</strong> — some government-related renewal fees are marginally lower in Abu Dhabi, though this varies by service and changes over time.</li>
</ul>
<p>And where it's comparable or higher: waterfront and island living (Al Reem, Al Raha, Saadiyat) prices at a premium similar to Dubai Marina or Downtown, and premium private schools in Abu Dhabi can match or exceed Dubai fee levels at the top end.</p>

<h2>Relocating Between the Two Emirates</h2>
<p>Some residents commute between Dubai and Abu Dhabi rather than choosing one, especially if a household has one earner working in each city. If that's your situation, the toll costs (Salik and Darb combined) and highway fuel spending deserve their own budget line — a daily Dubai–Abu Dhabi commute is a materially different transport cost than a within-city commute, and it's easy to underestimate until you see it itemized against a full month of fuel and toll receipts.</p>

